## Problem

SQL beginners can read a `CREATE TABLE` statement and still not see the shape of
the schema it belongs to. The relationships are spread across foreign-key clauses
in different statements, and "why is your schema like this?" is a question best
answered with a picture.

## Approach

Paste DDL (or upload a `.sql` file); get back a drawn schema plus a list of
problems. No account, no backend — it is a static bundle that does all its work
in the browser.

## Architecture

Four stages. The DDL is tokenised with `js-tokens` and the token stream is walked
into a domain model: `Database` → `Schema` → `Table` → `Column`, with
`ForeignKey` and `ColumnType` attached. A `Validator` then walks the built model
and raises `Problem` objects — `MissingPrimaryKeyProblem`, `NoForeignKeyProblem`,
`MultipleRootsProblem` — each carrying a fix hint. Layout finds the root tables
(the ones nothing points at), walks foreign keys recursively to assign each table
a depth, and splits into separate trees when there is more than one root.
Rendering draws table cards as Bootstrap markup positioned by depth, with
`leader-line-new` drawing the connectors and redrawing them on drag and scroll.

## Decisions and trade-offs

**A hand-written token walk instead of a parser generator.** ANTLR with a real
SQL grammar would handle far more DDL than this does. It would also have made
the parsing step an opaque dependency, and understanding parsing was the point of
the project. The subset it handles is small and the failure mode is explicit: an
invalid construct throws a `SyntaxError` carrying the offending text, which the
UI highlights in the textarea.

**Validation lives in the constructors.** `Schema`, `Table`, `Column` and
`ForeignKey` each enforce their own rules — valid name, no duplicates, known
type — when they are constructed. An invalid schema therefore cannot be built at
all, and the error messages fall out of the model rather than being assembled by
a separate checking pass.

**No framework.** The UI is written against the DOM API directly. For a project
whose purpose was to understand rendering, bundling and parsing without
abstraction in the way, a framework would have hidden the interesting half.

## Outcome

Finished and archived. Not currently deployed — `npm start` runs the whole thing
locally, since there is no server component to host. The parser and model are
covered by Mocha unit tests.
